### §10.2 STATUS after the subgroup-products theorem — and the real cost of Lemma 2

The blocker is GONE, and it was not what I said. I reported §10.2 blocked on "generation
by a SET" needing a new construction. Unpacking Judson's conclusion showed otherwise:

    "N contains all the 3-cycles (i,j,k) … these generate A_n; hence N = A_n"
    = every element is a product of 3-cycles (Lemma 1)
    + each of those 3-cycles is in N (the conjugation step)
    + N is closed under PRODUCTS OF RUNS                      <- the only missing piece

That third is now `std/subgroup/products.b4m`'s
`aProductOfSubgroupEntriesIsInTheSubgroup` — an induction on run length over the abstract
`inSubgroup`, reusable for any subgroup-generation argument. No generated-subgroup object.

**ALL FOUR identities VERIFY computationally** (a python trace over the five/six relevant
points, which is the check to run BEFORE writing any proof — it caught a mis-trace in
Lemma 1). And `simplify composeApply` unfolds a TWELVE-factor composite, so there is no
cap problem and no need for an N-factor helper (see the helper-treadmill entry above).

But the measured cost is larger than I estimated, because a 3-cycle is TWO transpositions:

| identity | RHS transposition factors | letters | point-cases | est. lines |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `(i,a,j) = (i,j,a)^2` | 4 | 3 | 4 | DONE (~180) |
| `(i,a,b) = (i,j,b)(i,j,a)^2` | 6 | 4 | 5 | ~345 |
| `(j,a,b) = (i,j,b)^2(i,j,a)` | 6 | 4 | 5 | ~345 |
| `(a,b,c) = (i,j,a)^2(i,j,c)(i,j,b)^2(i,j,a)` | **12** | 5 | 6 | ~504 |

So ~1200 more lines for the three identities, then the conjugation step, then Lemma 3's
five-way classification and its own conjugations. Judson's "(a,b,c) = …" is six 3-cycles =
twelve transpositions, not the eight I first wrote.

