### §10.2's REAL REQUIREMENT: generation by a SET (everything else is present)

Audited 2026-10-07, after TWO wrong answers about what std lacks. What is actually there:

| thought missing | actually in std |
|---|---|
| the SHAPE of a disjoint-cycle decomposition | `decomposesIntoDisjointCycles(f)` is structured: a `Seq w` of cycles, a count `m`, pairwise-disjoint supports, `productUpTo(w, m) = f`. Judson's `σ = τ·(a₁…a_r)` IS expressible — τ is the product of the other entries. |
| a cycle's LENGTH | `isACycle(g)` unfolds to `g = cycle(s, succ(p))`, so the length is a nameable witness and comparable with `less_than`. Verified by probe. |
| conjugation as RELABELLING | `conjugateOfACycle` (`std/permutation/cycle.b4m`) — `σ∘cycle(s,k)∘σ⁻¹ = cycle(t,k)` for ARBITRARY k, plus `conjugationIsMultiplicative` and `conjugateOfATransposition`. |

That last one matters most: it is exactly the machinery Lemma 3's VARIABLE-LENGTH
computations need (`(a₁…a_r)⁻¹τ⁻¹(a₁a₂a₃)τ(a₁…a_r)(a₁a₃a₂) = (a₁a₃a_r)` with r a
parameter), and it removes the one place Lemma 1's fixed-length method could not reach.

**THE GENUINE GAP — generation by a SET.** std has `inGenerated(g, x) = exists k: Int;
x = power(g, k)` (`std/group/generated.b4m`): the subgroup generated by ONE element. Both
Lemma 2 and Lemma 3 end on "these 3-cycles generate A_n, hence N = A_n", which needs
closure under products of MANY generators — a membership predicate containing a given
family and closed under op/inverse. That is a new construction, not vocabulary.

**ALSO NEEDED, and real: Lemma 3's classification.** std proves a decomposition EXISTS;
nothing says it falls into Judson's five shapes (a 3-cycle / a cycle of length > 3 / two
3-cycles / a 3-cycle times disjoint 2-cycles / two 2-cycles times an even number more).
Statable with the vocabulary above, but it is a genuine proof over cycle-length multisets
plus the parity constraint (σ ∈ A_n means an even transposition count).

So the state is: Lemma 2's three remaining identities and its conjugation are MECHANICAL
and unblocked; Lemma 2's CONCLUSION and all of Lemma 3 sit behind set-generation.

PROCESS: the two wrong answers both came from asserting absence without grepping. The
`std has no index` rule ("grep the distinctive TERM, not a guessed name") applies to
ABSENCE claims exactly as much as to lookups — and `conjugateOfACycle` was found by one
grep for `conjugat` after I had already said it was missing.

### PROBE WHETHER AN ACCELERANT ALREADY DOES IT, BEFORE WRITING A HELPER

I wrote three theorems — `twoFactorValue`, `fourFactorValue`, `sixFactorValue` — giving the
value of an N-factor composite at a point. All three were the SAME fact, `composeApply`
(`apply(compose(g,f), x) = apply(g, apply(f, x))`), applied a fixed number of times with
ONE parenthesization hardcoded. `twoFactorValue` was `composeApply` verbatim, which std's
alias rule forbids outright.

It was a treadmill: a seventh factor, or the same six grouped differently, would each have
needed another theorem. What actually does the job:

    [using simplify_quantified composeApply]   -- for a quantified statement
    [using simplify composeApply]              -- at a concrete call site

Either unfolds ANY nesting of ANY length in one step. Deleting the three helpers and
rewriting their 18 call sites removed 123 lines with no loss of content.

LESSON: before writing a helper theorem for a shape, check whether an accelerant handles
the shape. In `std/` accelerants are the preferred proof (the cheat-sheet says so); a
hand-written N-ary family is the thing to be suspicious of.

### A SCHEMA CANNOT RECURSE ON ITS OWN ARGUMENT (and the growing-term form HANGS)

Probed while looking for a way to collapse the per-identity repetition. Two shapes:

- `thm(n)` citing `thm(n)` — correctly rejected, `cyclic schema instantiation`.
- `thm(n)` citing `thm(prev(n))` — NOT rejected, and DIVERGES. `prev(n)` is a
  syntactically distinct term, so each instance gets a distinct name and the cycle check
  never fires; the argument grows (`prev(prev(n))`, …) instead of shrinking, so no base
  case is ever reached. Killed after 2.5 minutes of CPU on an 8-declaration file.

Why it cannot work: a schema is a MACRO monomorphized at the WRITTEN argument, with no
evaluation of `prev(succ(k)) -> k` at instantiation time. Terminating would require the
instantiator to reduce the argument, i.e. to compute — which is what `induction` does, and
why `std/peano.b4m`'s `induction` schema exists and transfers through models.

USER (2026-10-07): curiosity, not a requirement — "it's possible we will never need this."
Recorded because the growing-term form HANGS rather than diagnosing: if a schema ever does
need a depth or term-size cap, this is the case that motivates it.

### §10.2 PROGRESS — Lemma 1 is PROVED; what Lemma 2 costs

Judson's Lemma 1 ("A_n is generated by 3-cycles") is done, hole-free and strict, in
`std/permutation/alternating.b4m`. Its three identities:

| Judson | status |
|---|---|
| `(a,b)(a,b) = identity` | std's `transpositionIsAnInvolution`, already there |
| `(a,b)(c,d) = (a,c,b)(a,c,d)` | `aPairOfDisjointTranspositionsIsAProductOfThreeCycles` |
| `(a,b)(a,c) = (a,c,b)` | DEFINITIONAL — not a theorem (see below) |

Two findings that make the rest of the section tractable:

**A 3-CYCLE NEEDS NO NEW SORT.** std's cycle fold gives
`cycle(s,3) = (a0 a2) o (a0 a1) o ((a0 a0) o id)` (`cycleSucc` twice, then `cycleZero`),
and `transpositionOfAPointWithItself` kills the degenerate tail. So a 3-cycle IS a
composite of two transpositions sharing their first point — which is why Judson's third
identity is a reading of the definition, and why the second carries all the content.
Proved as `aThreeCycleIsTwoTranspositions`.

**THE METHOD IS EXTENSIONALITY, NOT CYCLE ALGEBRA.** Each identity is: expand both sides
to nested `apply`s (`twoFactorValue` / `fourFactorValue`), evaluate factor by factor with
`transpositionAtFirst`/`AtSecond`/`Elsewhere`, then `funcExtensionality` over a case split
on where the point sits. For identity (2) that is five cases (at a, b, c, d, elsewhere),
each ~60 lines but mechanical once the first is written.

TOOLCHAIN NOTES, all three cost a debugging round:
- `simplify` CANNOT close these joins — it orients each equation left-to-right and the
  middle step must run backward. `chain` is orientation-free and closes them immediately.
- `chain` REJECTS a repeated ref (`chain a b a` is a label-shadowing error); state the
  step once.
- `tautology` sees `a != c` and `c != a` as UNRELATED atoms, so disequality symmetry is a
  real step. std has NO such theorem; `disequalityIsSymmetric` is proved locally in
  `alternating.b4m` and WANTS LIFTING to `std/element.b4m`.

**WHAT LEMMA 2 COSTS.** "If N contains a 3-cycle then N = A_n" needs four more identities
rewriting an arbitrary 3-cycle in terms of the fixed-`(i,j)` family:

    (i,a,j) = (i,j,a)^2
    (i,a,b) = (i,j,b)(i,j,a)^2
    (j,a,b) = (i,j,b)^2(i,j,a)
    (a,b,c) = (i,j,a)^2(i,j,c)(i,j,b)^2(i,j,a)

plus the conjugation `[(i,j)(a,k)](i,j,a)^2[(i,j)(a,k)]^-1 = (i,j,k)`. In transposition
composites the last identity is EIGHT factors, so it needs a `sixFactorValue` /
`eightFactorValue` in the same style, and each identity splits on more points (up to five
distinct letters). Same method, more volume — no new machinery, and no new primitive.
Estimate from Lemma 1's shape: ~6 helper theorems and ~5 point-cases per identity.

### SIMPLICITY is stated per-subgroup, because there is no sort of subgroups

`isSimpleWitness` in `std/group/normal.b4m` is
`isTrivialSubgroup or isTheWholeGroup` — the property of THE subgroup that file carries, not
"for all normal subgroups N of G". Quantifying over subgroups needs a sort of subgroups, the
same limit Chapter 9 hit for ≅ (CHAPTER9-PAINPOINTS: "2b4m can state a theory and MODEL one
theory in another, but cannot talk about theories as objects").

The workable shape is the one std already uses for subgroups generally: a consumer declares
its own membership predicate and proves the disjunction for it. `std/subgroup.b4m` has a
second predicate `inSubgroup2` for exactly this reason (two subgroups at once), which is the
declare-what-you-need pattern rather than a limitation.

## WHAT WORKED, worth keeping

### A coset is its MEMBERSHIP PREDICATE, and Judson's equalities survive that

There is no sort of subsets of a group, so §10.1's `gN = Ng` is extensional agreement of two
predicates and `aN = bN` is `inSubgroup(a⁻¹b)` (§6.1's
`cosetsCoincideIffQuotientIsInTheSubgroup`). Both are exactly how Judson USES them — he never
needs the coset as an object, only membership and equality — so the transliteration is
faithful rather than a workaround. `coset.b4m` develops only the left coset (Lagrange needs
one, and the proofs mirror), so `inRightCoset` is defined in `normal.b4m`.

### `--axioms` does not see inside a `simplify` rewrite set

Dropping aliases that `--axioms` reported unused removed `invProduct`, which IS cited — by
the `simplify` step in `theFactorOperationIsWellDefined`. The check caught it immediately
("reference not found"), but worth knowing: `--axioms` attributes what a proof RESTS on, and
a rewrite named in a `simplify` set does not show up there. Same happened with
`subgroupHasIdentity` when §10.2's work later needed it.

### STAGE A LONG REWRITE CHAIN — nine equations exceeded `chain`'s search

The conjugation step needed nine equations to get from `sigma(i,j,a)^2 sigma^-1` to
`(i,j,k)`. One `chain` with all nine failed ("cannot connect … from the cited equations"),
and so did one `simplify` (two of the links must run backward). What worked: SPLIT at the
orientation boundary —

    @conjugate-is-the-square | … = (j,i,k)^2   [using simplify <the seven left-to-right links>]
    @conclusion-conjugate    | … = (i,j,k)     [using chain conjugate-is-the-square <the two reversible ones>]

`simplify` for the stretch that is all left-to-right, `chain` for the short tail that
needs reorientation. Neither tactic has to span the whole distance.

### `specialize` NEEDS TERM ARGS FOR BINDERS THAT FOLLOW A PREMISE

`conjugationIsMultiplicative` is `forall sigma; invertible(sigma) -> forall f, g; …`.
Writing `specialize conjugationIsMultiplicative(sigma) sigma-is-a-permutation` leaves `f`
and `g` UNBOUND, and the diagnostic shows the remainder as a `forall b1: Fn; forall b2: Fn;
…` — accurate but easy to misread as a shape mismatch. The fix is the rule
CHAPTER6-PAINPOINTS already records for `fullOrderGenerates`: put EVERY term in the parens
regardless of where its binder sits, `specialize thm(sigma, f, g) <premises>`.

### A STATEMENT'S HYPOTHESIS MAY BE UNUSED BY ITS PROOF, AND THAT IS AN ERROR

The conjugation statement carried `a != k` because Judson's `sigma = (i,j)(a,k)` is a
product of two genuine transpositions. The proof never needs it —
`transpositionIsAPermutation` holds for a degenerate transposition too — so the
unused-fact check rejected it and the hypothesis had to come out. Worth knowing when
transcribing: the book states distinctness for mathematical context, and the formal proof
only takes what it cites.

### REACH FOR `assoc(lemma)` WHEN THE WORK IS REGROUPING — not hand-written assoc steps

Corollary of the entry below, and of the N-helper treadmill above. Proving
`sigma o sigma = id` for `sigma = (i,j)(a,k)` needs the product regrouped so the middle
pair is adjacent, and I wrote FOUR hand instantiations of `composeAssoc` to do it —
`composeAssoc(h, g, f)` gives `(h∘g)∘f = h∘(g∘f)`, so each one has to be aimed by hand and
the error when you miss is a whole-term expected-vs-got.

`[using assoc(composeAssoc)]` right-nests BOTH sides and compares, so each regrouping is
one step with nothing to aim. Every one of the four collapsed.

The rule: `simplify` rewrites to a normal form and will not reassociate to expose a
cancellation (below); `assoc(lemma)` exists precisely for the reassociation. If the step is
"the same factors, differently grouped", that is the tactic.

### `simplify` will not reassociate to expose a cancellation

`op(c, op(inverse(c), d)) = d` fails as a `simplify` goal even with `grpAssoc`,
`grpInverseRight` and `grpIdentityLeft` in the set — it will not reassociate the nest to put
`c` next to `inverse(c)`. Four explicit steps (regroup, cancel, identity, chain) do it, after
which `simplify` closes the surrounding algebra happily. Consistent with the cheat-sheet's
framing of `simplify` as "rewriting to a shared normal form" rather than a search.
